## Methods ##

## 1. RNA-Seq Data Collection

Six publicly available RNA-seq datasets were selected from the NCBI Sequence Read Archive (SRA) under study accession **GSE113333**.

The selected samples represented two experimental conditions:

- **Control:** Uninfected human intestinal tissue
- **Infected:** *Salmonella Typhi*-infected human intestinal tissue

### Selected RNA-Seq Samples

| Sample | Condition | Run Accession |
|---|---|---|
| Control Replicate 1 | Uninfected | SRR7029701 |
| Control Replicate 2 | Uninfected | SRR7029702 |
| Control Replicate 3 | Uninfected | SRR7029703 |
| Infected Replicate 1 | *S. Typhi*-infected | SRR7029705 |
| Infected Replicate 2 | *S. Typhi*-infected | SRR7029706 |
| Infected Replicate 3 | *S. Typhi*-infected | SRR7029707 |

The FASTQ files were imported into **Galaxy** and organized in one Galaxy History for downstream RNA-seq analysis.



## 2. Raw Read Quality Control Using FastQC

Each of the six FASTQ files was analyzed using **FastQC** in Galaxy.

FastQC was used to evaluate:

1. Total number of sequencing reads
2. Per-base sequence quality
3. Per-sequence quality
4. Per-base sequence content
5. Per-sequence GC content
6. Sequence duplication levels
7. Overrepresented sequences
8. Adapter content
9. Sequence length

The FastQC results were examined based on **PASS**, **WARNING**, and **FAIL** classifications.

The six samples produced approximately **20.8–24.4 million reads** and generally showed high sequencing quality. All six samples passed the adapter-content assessment.

Warnings or failures were observed for some samples in per-base sequence content, GC content, sequence duplication, and overrepresented sequences.



## 3. MultiQC Quality-Control Summary

The six FastQC reports were combined using **MultiQC** in Galaxy.

MultiQC was used to compare the quality-control results across all six RNA-seq samples.

The following metrics were examined:

- Sequencing read number
- General sequence quality
- GC-content distribution
- Sequence duplication
- Overrepresented sequences
- Adapter contamination

The MultiQC summary was used to identify samples with unusual quality-control patterns before read mapping.



## 4. Read Trimming Assessment

Read trimming was **not performed** in this re-analysis.

The FastQC and MultiQC results showed generally high per-base sequence quality and no significant adapter contamination. Therefore, the original untrimmed FASTQ files were retained for downstream alignment.

This approach was also kept consistent with the reported workflow of the original study.



